<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hangul">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hangul</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1443</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Hangeul, Korean alphabet, Hunminjeongeum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hangul is the Korean alphabet, created in 1443 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> of Joseon and promulgated in 1446 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum">Hunminjeongeum</a>, &quot;the proper sounds for instructing the people&quot;. Its letters represent consonants and vowels and are grouped into syllable blocks. Long overshadowed by classical Chinese among the elite, it became the national script of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hangul.jpg" alt="Hangul" width="320" height="274" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: metalslick · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hangul_chosongul_fontembed.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
